---
import Lab from '../layouts/Lab.astro';
import { releases } from '../data/site';
import { fmtDate, v } from '../lib/format';

const a = releases.autobot.latest;
const i = releases.invincible.latest;

const sovereign = [
  ['Runs on what you own', "the phone's NPU and GPU, a desktop, a GPU box. A server you rent runs the same software, but it is someone else's machine. A cloud model is a choice you make per call, never a requirement."],
  ['Offline by default', 'every app starts with the network off. A kill switch rejects a request before DNS or a socket; the route (direct, Tor, SOCKS5) is yours to set.'],
  ['No accounts, no telemetry', 'nothing phones home. Crash reports and analytics do not exist. The log of what left the device is on the device.'],
  ['Source you can read', 'free software, signed releases, published hashes. Build it yourself and compare.'],
  ['Open formats', 'your sessions, parts and receipts in files you can move, back up and read without us.'],
  ['Checked, not trusted', 'what an agent made is scored by verifiers out of process and written to a ledger anyone can replay.'],
  ['Still there tomorrow', 'the source, the releases and the data are mirrored and archived where no single company, host or country can take them down.'],
];

const work = [
  ['Advocacy', 'ongoing', 'the rules above, written down, argued for, and applied to every project in the stack before it ships'],
  ['Signed releases', 'shipping', 'Autobot and Invincible publish checksums, attestations and a pinned signing key; every release is reproducible or says why not'],
  ['Security across the repos', 'in progress', 'one policy for secrets, dependencies, supply chain and disclosure across every WCKD repository; internal reviews are in, the independent review of pairing and remote access is still to come'],
  ['Mirrors', 'planned', 'every repository and every release mirrored outside GitHub, on infrastructure the community runs, with the same signatures'],
  ['Archives', 'planned', 'model weights, datasets and specifications the stack depends on, archived with hashes so a vanished upstream does not break a build'],
  ['The verifier kernel', 'L1 · one draft domain', 'the tool the org uses to check that a thing got better, not just different'],
];

const stack = [
  { name: 'autobot', what: 'private AI on Android and iOS, and the agent in your terminal', state: `v${v(a.tag)} · ${fmtDate(a.published)}`, href: 'https://autobot.sh' },
  { name: 'invincible', what: 'the node you own: one gateway for every model you run, on your hardware and across the machines you own, frontier models by policy, a signed receipt for every call', state: `v${v(i.tag)} · ${fmtDate(i.published)}`, href: 'https://invincible.sh' },
  { name: 'niobium', what: 'a client for your AI bots, built for Hermes, in pixel art', state: 'v1.1 · v2 in development', href: 'https://niobium.sh' },
  { name: 'omnie', what: 'an IDE for development on iPad and iPhone', state: 'TestFlight', href: 'https://omnie.sh' },
  { name: 'nightfall', html: 'AI-native CAD on exact NURBS; archived: the app continues as <a href="https://cad.polykrom.app" rel="noopener">Polykrom</a>, the kernel as <a href="https://github.com/wckdboy/polycore" rel="noopener">PolyCore</a>', state: 'archived', href: 'https://nightfall.sh' },
  { name: 'sovware', what: 'the verifier kernel: programs, verifiers, gates and the ledger', state: 'L1 · one draft domain', href: 'https://github.com/wckdboy/sovware' },
];

const status = [
  ['kernel', 'working', 'verify, eval, compare, ledger. conformance L1'],
  ['schemas', '7', 'strict: an unknown field is an error'],
  ['mechanical gate', 'draft', '4 verifiers, 3 programs. the spec asks for 30 and 10 held out'],
  ['circuits', 'not started', 'blocked on a stable KiCad report contract'],
  ['software', 'not started', 'the recursive domain: prompts and routing as programs'],
  ['sandbox', 'not started', 'timeouts only; landlock and seccomp next'],
  ['fabrication', 'closed', 'a human signature; the kernel has no path to a machine'],
];
---

<Lab
  path="/"
  current=""
  og="/og/sovware.png"
  description="Sovware is the non-profit community behind WCKD's software: it advocates for sovereign software and hardware and makes sure the stack actually is, with mirrors, archives, security across every repository and a verifier kernel."
>
  <h1>sovware</h1>
  <p class="lede">The non-profit community behind the stack. Sovware advocates for sovereign software and hardware, and makes sure the things WCKD ships actually are: mirrored, archived, secured and checked.</p>

  <dl class="facts">
    <div><dt>What</dt><dd>a non-profit community</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Keeps</dt><dd>mirrors, archives, the security policy</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Checks with</dt><dd>the verifier kernel</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Licenses</dt><dd>Apache-2.0, MIT</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <section id="sovereign">
    <h2>What sovereign means here</h2>
    <p>A model you rent on someone else's machine is a service. It can change, be rate-limited, read your data or go away. Sovereign software runs on hardware you control, keeps its state in files you can read, and treats the network as a tool you switch on, not a condition of use. Sovereign hardware is the machine you already have: the phone in your pocket with its NPU, the desktop under the desk, the GPU box in the closet. A server you rent and can leave is useful, but it is someone else's hardware. And sovereign means it is still there tomorrow, whatever happens to a host, a company or a country.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>In practice</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {sovereign.map(([rule, how]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{rule}</strong></td><td>{how}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <blockquote><p>Saying a thing is sovereign is marketing. Sovware is the part that checks, and keeps the copies.</p></blockquote>
  </section>

  <section id="work">
    <h2>What the org does</h2>
    <p>The projects build; Sovware makes sure what they build stays sovereign. It holds the rules, keeps the copies, runs the security policy across every repository, and uses the kernel to say whether a change was an improvement.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Duty</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {work.map(([duty, state, detail]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{duty}</strong></td><td>{state} <span class="small">· {detail}</span></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">States are the org's own reading. "Planned" means not started; a mirror that is not yet live is not listed as one.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="stack">
    <h2>The stack</h2>
    <p>Six projects from one lab, on one brand kit, under one set of rules. The phone runs the agent; the node runs the models; the org keeps all of it honest and available.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {stack.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong><a href={p.href} rel="noopener">{p.name}</a></strong></td>
              <td>{'html' in p && p.html ? <Fragment set:html={p.html} /> : p.what} <span class="small">· {p.state}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">Autobot pairs with Invincible over Noise and runs the node's agent, approvals and receipts from the phone. Invincible routes the models and signs the receipts. Omnie is where the code is written and reviewed. Nightfall, the first CAD domain for the kernel, is archived; CAD continues in <a href="https://cad.polykrom.app" rel="noopener">Polykrom</a> (<a href="https://github.com/wckdboy/polykrom" rel="noopener">source</a>), and its kernel work in <a href="https://github.com/wckdboy/polycore" rel="noopener">PolyCore</a>. Niobium is the client for a Hermes agent you host.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="kernel">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Checked, not trusted</h2>
      <span class="label">The verifier kernel</span>
    </div>
    <p>An agent can produce a part, a board, a program or a plan. It cannot tell you whether the thing is right, and a better model does not close that gap. The kernel is the org's instrument for that: every artifact is a program with source you can read, parameters with units and claims with sources; every program is scored by verifiers that run out of process and return a verdict, a score and a cost; every run goes into a hash-chained ledger anyone can replay.</p>
    <pre><code>program ─▶ verifiers ─▶ gate ─▶ ledger
   ▲                                 │
   └───────── mutation ◀─────────────┘</code></pre>
    <p>Improvement then has a definition: the gate passes, the score does not fall on a set the agent never saw, and the cost does not rise. A design that is cheaper and fails the gate is a change. The tool says so, and keeps the record.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {status.map(([part, state, detail]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{part}</strong></td><td>{state} <span class="small">· {detail}</span></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small">Nothing here is admitted for fabrication. The mechanical gate stays a draft until it has the corpus the specification asks for: 30 programs, 10 held out, 5 known-bad. Source: <a href="https://github.com/wckdboy/sovware" rel="noopener">wckdboy/sovware</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="rules">
    <h2>The rules that matter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No claim without a source. A citation that cannot be resolved is a bug.</li>
      <li>An errored verifier is a refusal. Missing evidence is not a pass.</li>
      <li>Statistical never gates. A score that cannot state its uncertainty has no vote.</li>
      <li>Nothing moves matter without a human signature. The kernel cannot call a printer, and neither can an agent.</li>
      <li>No tuning on the held-out set. The set is hashed before the first run.</li>
      <li>No single point of removal. Source, releases and data have a copy the org controls.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Lab>
